 The diagnosis (collapse $\to$ mechanism $\to$ minimal fix) and the controls that distinguish it from prior MoE-collapse threads structure our three contributions:
 \begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2em, itemsep=0.15em, topsep=0.2em]
 \item \textbf{Problem formulation.} We frame routing collapse on instance-normalized TSFMs as a router-\emph{input} failure rather than the router-\emph{optimization} failure that prior MoE work targets~\citep{fedus2022switch, zoph2022stmoe, wang2025remoe, zhou2022expertchoice}, naming it \emph{normalization-induced routing collapse}, and introduce a signal-ratio statistic $R(\mathcal{D})$ that quantifies dataset vulnerability before training. The dichotomy is empirical: a $720$-run sweep across $11$ optimization-side rescues recovers at most $10.9\%$ MSE, $2.7\times$ worse than the input-side intervention this framing prescribes.
-\item \textbf{Mechanistic finding.} Eight causal controls (RevIN ablation, BatchNorm/GroupNorm swaps, router-input interpolation, MOMENT-vs-Timer-XL backbone contrast, vision cross-modality, $(\mu,\sigma)$ re-injection, AdaMix-Raw, Residual-IA) converge on the encoder-internal $(\mu,\sigma)$-stripping normalizer as the single cause; a mutual-information decomposition (Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}) formalizes the loss, and $R(\mathcal{D})$ predicts the failure at Spearman $\rho{=}{-}0.88$ ($p{<}0.002$) across three normalization families, five backbones, and a vision modality, including boundary cases it correctly does not predict (Traffic, $R{=}0.14$).
-\item \textbf{Method.} We propose RR-MoA, the minimal causal intervention prescribed by the diagnosis: change only the router's input from normalized hidden states to $\mathbf{X}_\text{raw}$, with experts, optimizer, and frozen backbone unchanged. RR-MoA wins $54/54$ cells against AdaMix and is not subsumed by naive $(\mu,\sigma)$ re-injection ($+42$--$239\%$ MSE; $11/18$ cells re-collapse). Two architecturally distinct variants confirm the principle generalizes: \textbf{SR-MoA} (self-routed, no external router; $+13$--$42\%$ over RR-MoA on all 6 datasets) and \textbf{Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}} (expert-level raw-input dual-stream; matches or beats DLinear on $6/6$ datasets, $107/123$ cells across six backbones).
+\item \textbf{Experimental results.} Eight causal controls (RevIN ablation, BatchNorm/GroupNorm swaps, router-input interpolation, MOMENT-vs-Timer-XL backbone contrast, vision cross-modality, $(\mu,\sigma)$ re-injection, AdaMix-Raw, Residual-IA) converge on the encoder-internal $(\mu,\sigma)$-stripping normalizer as the single cause; a mutual-information decomposition (Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}) formalizes the loss, and $R(\mathcal{D})$ predicts the failure at Spearman $\rho{=}{-}0.88$ ($p{<}0.002$) across three normalization families, five backbones, and a vision modality, including boundary cases it correctly does not predict (Traffic, $R{=}0.14$).
+\item \textbf{Causal intervention.} We propose RR-MoA, the minimal intervention prescribed by the diagnosis: change only the router's input from normalized hidden states to $\mathbf{X}_\text{raw}$, with experts, optimizer, and frozen backbone unchanged. RR-MoA wins $54/54$ cells against AdaMix and is not subsumed by naive $(\mu,\sigma)$ re-injection ($+42$--$239\%$ MSE; $11/18$ cells re-collapse). Two architecturally distinct variants confirm the principle generalizes: \textbf{SR-MoA} (self-routed, no external router; $+13$--$42\%$ over RR-MoA on all 6 datasets) and \textbf{Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}} (expert-level raw-input dual-stream; matches or beats DLinear on $6/6$ datasets, $107/123$ cells across six backbones).
 \end{itemize}

